    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.ai\n\n### Description\n\nnvim 0.12 adds treesitter incremental node object selection, using `an` and `in` as well as using `]n` and `[n` for next and previous nodes and `<number>]n` for selecting across a number of nodes. This conflicts with the default `nvim.mini` \"next\" object modifier.\n\nOne option could be to use capital letters `N` and `L`  for \"next\" and \"last\" instead.\n\nAn ideal solution might be to use `]` and `[`, matching the tree-sitter bindings and working along side them, but I don't know whether this is possible.",
